:"Cyphotilapia frontosa","relation":"maximum body length","tail":"33 cm TL"},{"head":"Ophthalmotilapia ventralis","relation":"breeding compatibility classification","tail":"Suitable for group breeding (harem required)"},{"head":"Ophthalmotilapia ventralis","relation":"breeding compatibility reason","tail":"Maternal mouthbrooder that must be kept in a harem — one male with several females. Males highly aggressive toward other males. Harem structure essential to dissipate courtship aggression. Different color morphs must not be mixed as they hybridize freely."},{"head":"Neolamprologus pulcher","relation":"breeding compatibility classification","tail":"Suitable for group breeding (cooperative breeder)"},{"head":"Neolamprologus pulcher","relation":"breeding compatibility reason","tail":"One of the best-known cooperative breeders among cichlids. Lives in permanent social groups of one breeding pair + 1–14 helpers of both sexes. Group breeding is biologically essential. Helpers assist with brood care, territory defense, and predator vigilance. Shows increased aggression toward unfamiliar conspecifics (dear enemy effect)."},{"head":"Neolamprologus helianthus","relation":"breeding compatibility classification","tail":"Needs careful management (pair-forming, aggressive to conspecifics)"},{"head":"Neolamprologus helianthus","relation":"breeding compatibility reason","tail":"Territorial cave spawner very aggressive toward conspecifics. Start with group of 6+ juveniles for natural pair formation; subdominants typically not tolerated and must be removed. Once dominant pair forms, they bond for life. Multiple generations of fry can coexist with parents (Brichardi complex behavior). Multiple adult pairs require very large tank."},{"head":"Tropheus moorii","relation":"breeding compatibility classification","tail":"Suitable for group breeding (large colony mandatory)"},{"head":"Tropheus moorii","relation":"breeding compatibility reason","tail":"Maternal mouthbrooder that must be kept in large groups of 15–20+ individuals. In smaller groups (5–6), fish may fight to the death. Large colony size essential to distribute aggression and establish stable pecking order. 6–10 females per male recommended. New individuals must never be introduced into an established colony."},{"head":"Cyphotilapia frontosa","relation":"breeding compatibility classification","tail":"Suitable for group breeding (harem/colony structure)"},{"head":"Cyphotilapia frontosa","relation":"breeding compatibility reason","tail":"Gregarious maternal mouthbrooder; should be kept in groups of at least 5, ideally 10+. Males polygynous, each needing 3–4 females. Unlike many cichlids, males do not hold distinct territories — they form a dominance hierarchy. Females brood for 35–50+ days and practice buccal feeding. Sexual maturity takes several years."},{"head":"Ophthalmotilapia ventralis","relation":"first naming literature title","tail":"Report on the collection of fishes made by Mr. J. E. S. Moore in Lake Tanganyika during his expedition, 1895–96"},{"head":"Ophthalmotilapia ventralis","relation":"first naming literature journal","tail":"Transactions of the Zoological Society of London"},{"head":"Ophthalmotilapia ventralis","relation":"first naming literature volume/pages","tail":"Vol. 15, Part 1, No. 1, pp. 1–30, Plates 1–8"},{"head":"Ophthalmotilapia ventralis","relation":"first naming literature year","tail":"1898"},{"head":"Tropheus moorii","relation":"first naming literature title","tail":"Report on the fishes recently obtained by Mr. J. E. S. Moore in Lake Tanganyika"},{"head":"Tropheus moorii","relation":"first naming literature journal","tail":"Proceedings of the Zoological Society of London"},{"head":"Tropheus moorii","relation":"first naming literature volume/pages","tail":"Vol. 1898, pp. 494–497"},{"head":"Tropheus moorii","relation":"first naming literature year","tail":"1898"},{"head":"Cyphotilapia frontosa","relation":"first naming literature title","tail":"Fourth contribution to the ichthyology of Lake Tanganyika.—Report on the collection of fishes made by Dr. W. A. Cunnington during the Third Tanganyika Expedition, 1904–1905"},{"head":"Cyphotilapia frontosa","relation":"first naming literature journal","tail":"Transactions of the Zoological Society of London"},{"head":"Cyphotilapia frontosa","relation":"first naming literature volume/pages","tail":"Vol. 17, Part 6, No. 1, pp. 537–601, Plates 30–41"},{"head":"Cyphotilapia frontosa","relation":"first naming literature year","tail":"1906"},{"head":"Cyphotilapia frontosa","relation":"first naming literature DOI","tail":"10.1111/j.1469-7998.1906.tb07891.x"},{"head":"Cyphotilapia frontosa","relation":"first naming literature language","tail":"English"},{"head":"Neolamprologus pulcher","relation":"first naming literature title","tail":"Three new species and two new subspecies of the genus Lamprologus, Cichlid fishes of Lake Tanganyika"},{"head":"Neolamprologus pulcher","relation":"first naming literature journal","tail":"Bulletin de l'Institut Royal des Sciences Naturelles de Belgique"},{"head":"Neolamprologus pulcher","relation":"first naming literature volume/pages","tail":"Vol. 28, No. 50, pp. 1–16"},{"head":"Neolamprologus pulcher","relation":"first naming literature year","tail":"1952"},{"head":"Neolamprologus pulcher","relation":"first naming literature language","tail":"English (in a bilingual French/Dutch journal)"},{"head":"Neolamprologus pulcher","relation":"first naming literature publisher","tail":"Institut Royal des Sciences Naturelles de Belgique, Brussels"},{"head":"Neolamprologus pulcher","relation":"first naming literature based on specimens from","tail":"Belgian Hydrobiological Mission (1946–1947) and Christy Collection (1920–1927)"},{"head":"Neolamprologus pulcher","relation":"first naming literature type locality","tail":"Kasanga, Tanzania"},{"head":"Neolamprologus pulcher","relation":"first naming literature other taxa described","tail":"Lamprologus sexfasciatus sp. 